<article class="guide"><h2>What a prenup can and cannot do</h2><p>A prenuptial agreement can record arrangements about assets and finances, but enforceability and permissible terms differ by jurisdiction. Courts may consider fairness, disclosure, circumstances and the needs of family members. This calculator does not evaluate validity, spousal support or children's financial needs.</p>
<h2>How property rules vary</h2><p>In California, a common starting point for community property is equal division, but classification and exceptions matter. In New York, equitable distribution means fair division rather than a guaranteed fifty-fifty split. In England and Wales, courts consider individual circumstances when making financial orders; prenuptial agreements are not automatically binding.</p>
<h2>How to use this scenario simulator</h2><p>Enter each partner's separately designated assets, joint assets and liabilities. Choose a default-law illustration percentage and the split a couple might propose in an agreement. Review the mathematical difference, then discuss any assumptions and legal constraints with an independent qualified lawyer in the relevant jurisdiction.</p>
